A pipeline is a transformation contract
The pipe |&gt; passes the object on the left into the first argument of the function on the right. The older %&gt;% operator from magrittr is also common in existing code. Use either consistently, and read the pipeline from top to bottom.
library(dplyr)

mtcars |&gt;
  filter(cyl &gt;= 6) |&gt;
  select(mpg, cyl, hp, wt) |&gt;
  arrange(desc(mpg))
At each line, ask: “What is the row grain now?” and “How many rows and columns should I expect?” Insert count() or glimpse() checks while developing.

A complete transformation trace
library(dplyr)

mtcars_summary &lt;- mtcars |&gt;
  as_tibble(rownames = "model") |&gt;
  mutate(
    transmission = if_else(am == 1, "Manual", "Automatic"),
    weight_1000lb = wt
  ) |&gt;
  group_by(transmission) |&gt;
  summarize(
    vehicles = n(),
    average_mpg = mean(mpg),
    average_weight = mean(weight_1000lb),
    .groups = "drop"
  ) |&gt;
  arrange(desc(average_mpg))
The output has one row per transmission type. That sentence is part of the analysis, not a comment added after the fact.

Live lab: wrangle airquality
Use the month selector to compare ozone and solar radiation. Notice that the means use na.rm = TRUE; your written interpretation should also report that the available observations differ by variable.
#| '!! shinylive warning !!': |
#|   shinylive does not work in self-contained HTML documents.
#|   Please set `embed-resources: false` in your metadata.
#| standalone: true
#| components: [editor, viewer]

library(shiny)
library(dplyr)
library(tidyr)

air &lt;- airquality |&gt;
  mutate(Month = as.integer(Month), Day = as.integer(Day))

ui &lt;- fluidPage(
  titlePanel("Air-quality wrangling lab"),
  sidebarLayout(
    sidebarPanel(
      selectInput("month", "Month", choices = sort(unique(air$Month)), selected = 5),
      helpText("Change the month and inspect how missing values affect usable counts.")
    ),
    mainPanel(
      h4("Summary by measure"),
      tableOutput("summary"),
      h4("First observations"),
      tableOutput("preview")
    )
  )
)

server &lt;- function(input, output, session) {
  selected &lt;- reactive({
    air |&gt;
      filter(Month == as.integer(input$month))
  })

  output$summary &lt;- renderTable({
    selected() |&gt;
      summarize(
        days = n(),
        ozone_observed = sum(!is.na(Ozone)),
        mean_ozone = mean(Ozone, na.rm = TRUE),
        solar_observed = sum(!is.na(Solar.R)),
        mean_solar = mean(Solar.R, na.rm = TRUE)
      ) |&gt;
      pivot_longer(everything(), names_to = "measure", values_to = "value")
  })

  output$preview &lt;- renderTable({
    selected() |&gt;
      select(Day, Ozone, Solar.R, Wind, Temp) |&gt;
      head(8)
  })
}

shinyApp(ui, server)

Interactive project lab
The synthetic table below is deliberately small enough to audit by hand. Change the region selector, inspect the aggregation, and test whether your summary remains correct when you alter one value.
#| '!! shinylive warning !!': |
#|   shinylive does not work in self-contained HTML documents.
#|   Please set `embed-resources: false` in your metadata.
#| standalone: true
#| components: [editor, viewer]

library(shiny)
library(dplyr)
library(ggplot2)

sales_monthly &lt;- data.frame(
  region = rep(c("North", "South", "East", "West"), each = 3),
  month = rep(c("Jan", "Feb", "Mar"), times = 4),
  sales_usd = c(118000, 124000, 131000, 106000, 111000, 108000, 126000, 132000, 139000, 92000, 98000, 101000),
  target_usd = c(120000, 120000, 125000, 115000, 115000, 120000, 120000, 130000, 135000, 110000, 110000, 115000),
  orders = c(410, 430, 445, 390, 400, 405, 420, 440, 470, 350, 370, 380),
  sales_rep_count = c(12, 12, 13, 11, 11, 11, 12, 12, 12, 10, 10, 10)
)

ui &lt;- fluidPage(
  titlePanel("Regional attainment project lab"),
  sidebarLayout(
    sidebarPanel(
      selectInput("region", "Region", choices = c("All", sort(unique(sales_monthly$region)))),
      helpText("The metric is total sales divided by total target.")
    ),
    mainPanel(
      tableOutput("summary"),
      plotOutput("plot", height = "430px")
    )
  )
)

server &lt;- function(input, output, session) {
  selected &lt;- reactive({
    if (input$region == "All") sales_monthly else sales_monthly |&gt; filter(region == input$region)
  })

  output$summary &lt;- renderTable({
    selected() |&gt;
      group_by(region) |&gt;
      summarize(
        months = n(),
        total_sales = sum(sales_usd),
        total_target = sum(target_usd),
        attainment = total_sales / total_target,
        total_orders = sum(orders),
        .groups = "drop"
      )
  })

  output$plot &lt;- renderPlot({
    sales_monthly |&gt;
      group_by(region) |&gt;
      summarize(attainment = sum(sales_usd) / sum(target_usd), .groups = "drop") |&gt;
      mutate(region = reorder(region, attainment)) |&gt;
      ggplot(aes(region, attainment)) +
      geom_col(fill = "#176B87") +
      geom_hline(yintercept = 1, linetype = "dashed") +
      scale_y_continuous(labels = scales::percent_format(accuracy = 1)) +
      labs(x = NULL, y = "Attainment", title = "Regional attainment") +
      theme_minimal(base_size = 12)
  })
}

shinyApp(ui, server)

Live lab: EDA triage
The synthetic dataset contains a missing value and a high-value order. Use the controls to inspect the distribution and compare the mean with the median before deciding how to describe “typical” order value.
#| '!! shinylive warning !!': |
#|   shinylive does not work in self-contained HTML documents.
#|   Please set `embed-resources: false` in your metadata.
#| standalone: true
#| components: [editor, viewer]

library(shiny)
library(ggplot2)
library(dplyr)

orders &lt;- data.frame(
  order_id = 1:12,
  channel = c("Web", "Web", "Store", "Store", "Partner", "Web", "Store", "Partner", "Web", "Store", "Partner", "Web"),
  order_value = c(85, 92, 110, 130, 1250, 74, 98, 150, NA, 105, 180, 88),
  delivery_days = c(2, 3, 1, 2, 8, 2, 1, 6, 3, 2, 5, 2),
  cancelled = c(FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, FALSE, TRUE, FALSE, FALSE, FALSE)
)

ui &lt;- fluidPage(
  titlePanel("EDA triage lab"),
  sidebarLayout(
    sidebarPanel(
      selectInput("channel", "Channel", choices = c("All", sort(unique(orders$channel)))),
      checkboxInput("log", "Use a log y-axis", value = FALSE)
    ),
    mainPanel(
      tableOutput("metrics"),
      plotOutput("distribution", height = "400px")
    )
  )
)

server &lt;- function(input, output, session) {
  filtered &lt;- reactive({
    if (input$channel == "All") orders else orders |&gt; filter(channel == input$channel)
  })

  output$metrics &lt;- renderTable({
    filtered() |&gt;
      summarize(
        rows = n(),
        observed_values = sum(!is.na(order_value)),
        missing_values = sum(is.na(order_value)),
        mean_value = mean(order_value, na.rm = TRUE),
        median_value = median(order_value, na.rm = TRUE),
        p90_value = as.numeric(quantile(order_value, 0.90, na.rm = TRUE))
      )
  })

  output$distribution &lt;- renderPlot({
    p &lt;- ggplot(filtered(), aes(x = order_value)) +
      geom_histogram(bins = 8, na.rm = TRUE, fill = "#176B87", color = "white") +
      labs(title = "Order-value distribution", x = "Order value", y = "Orders") +
      theme_minimal(base_size = 12)
    if (isTRUE(input$log)) p &lt;- p + scale_x_log10()
    p
  })
}

shinyApp(ui, server)

A root-cause prompt
Act as a debugging investigator, not a code generator.

Business intent: produce one row per customer with total completed-order revenue.
Expected row count: equal to the number of customer IDs in customers.

Code:
customers |&gt; left_join(orders, by = "customer_id") |&gt;
  group_by(customer_id) |&gt; summarize(revenue = sum(order_value))

Complete error/output:
&lt;paste the exact console output here&gt;

Please return:
1. the most likely root cause;
2. two tests that would confirm or reject it;
3. the smallest code change;
4. one business-logic risk that could remain even if the code runs.
Do not rewrite the entire pipeline.
If the assistant proposes na.rm = TRUE, ask what population is being excluded and whether missing order values mean zero, unknown, or an invalid record. If it proposes inner_join(), ask which customers would disappear.

Sanity checks that belong in production code
stopifnot(nrow(customers) == n_distinct(customers$customer_id))

result &lt;- customers |&gt;
  left_join(orders, by = "customer_id") |&gt;
  group_by(customer_id) |&gt;
  summarize(
    revenue = sum(order_value, na.rm = TRUE),
    orders = n_distinct(order_id),
    .groups = "drop"
  )

stopifnot(nrow(result) == nrow(customers))
stopifnot(all(result$orders &gt;= 0))
Tests should encode the business contract, not just implementation details. A test that passes because a column exists is weaker than a test that confirms the output has one row per customer and no negative completed-order counts.

3. Connect a local project safely
For a local coding agent, start with a dedicated project directory containing the Quarto source, a README, and a .gitignore. Open only the directory needed for the task. Before allowing an agent to edit files:

confirm the current branch and working-tree status;
commit or copy any work you cannot easily reconstruct;
remove secrets and large raw extracts from the directory;
state which files are in scope and which must remain untouched;
ask the agent to show a plan or diff before broad changes.

For browser-based chat, paste a small file or a representative schema rather than an entire repository. For editor assistants, keep the active file focused and use comments to state the business question, expected grain, and acceptance tests.

This is a rigorous introduction to R for students who want to make defensible business decisions from data. The course treats R as a language for computational thinking: you will represent a question, define a data contract, transform observations, inspect evidence, design a visual argument, and translate the result for a stakeholder.
Modern analysts do not need to type every line from memory. They do need to understand enough R to direct an AI assistant, recognize a plausible-looking mistake, test a result, and explain the business logic. We move from syntax memorization to computational orchestration and verification.


No prior R programming is required. Students should be comfortable with percentages, averages, tables, and reading a short business case. Curiosity, careful note-taking, and a willingness to test assumptions matter more than prior coding experience.



By the end of the course, you should be able to:

read and write core R expressions involving names, vectors, tables, functions, and conditions;
construct dplyr and tidyr pipelines with explicit row grain, joins, missing-value rules, and denominators;
design ggplot2 graphics using the Grammar of Graphics and audience-appropriate annotation;
conduct EDA that checks distributions, missingness, outliers, and relationships before making a claim;
prompt Codex, Claude, ChatGPT, or another approved assistant using a schema-first specification;
debug AI-generated code by preserving errors, testing root causes, and applying small fixes;
write a three-sentence executive takeaway that is supported by the verified output.

